So the new #TrumpRx adds a new discount modality for drugs…unfortunately doesn’t address the root issue that US drug pricing is so astronomical that we have a patchwork quilt of basically coupon-clipping to try to make them somewhat affordable…

Let’s go through them, shall we?

TrumpRx by @realDonaldTrump - limited list of brand-name drugs for cash-paying patients. Several explicitly with limited time or # of fills. Acts like a government-branded coupon aggregator in many ways. 

@costplusdrugs by @mcuban - extensive list of generics for chronic diseases at low costs. Costs are transparent, stable and long-term. No brand name. No insurance required. 

@GoodRx - part of the underlying tech of TrumpRx, but on its own works as a discounted network of prices that @goodrx has negotiated with PBMs. Lots of generics, some brand names. Prices can be variable over time because @goodrx has to renegotiate rates. 

@amazon pharmacy - in many ways, vertically integrates coupon clipping into one flow. Contracts with insurance and also has its own cash pay prices. Auto-applies manufacturer coupons if available (brand-name drugs, insured patients). Throws on some Prime discounts when able on subscription bundling (especially for chronic generics). 

Manufacturer coupons - did you know you can go to a drug company’s site and see if they have coupons for their brand name drugs? Many people don’t. Usually for commercially insured patients only (just decreases the patient portion of $), usually short term (a few fills). 

Semaglutide (Wegovy/Ozempic) brought @novonordisk over $38B a year, Humira got @abbvie $35B, and Eliquis @pfizer $22B a year. Discounts are nice…but maybe the fundamental issue is with the sticker price itself... 🤷🏻‍♀️

